## Migration: `Thread.Abort` Timeout → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Pattern Identified: **Timeout Enforcement**

Your code forcibly aborts a thread after a timeout. In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` and `Thread.ResetAbort()` is gone (`SYSLIB0006`).

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Task.WaitAsync throws OperationCanceledException on timeout
            return task.WaitAsync(cts.Token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }

    // Preferred: async version
    public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(
        Func<Task<T>> work, TimeSpan timeout, CancellationToken token = default)
    {
        using var cts = CancellationTokenSource.CreateLinkedTokenSource(token);
        cts.CancelAfter(timeout);

        try
        {
            return await Task.Run(() => work(), cts.Token).WaitAsync(cts.Token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested && !token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Mitigation |
|---------|------------|
| **Work doesn't actually stop** | Cooperative cancellation only works if `work` checks the token. If it's a tight CPU loop or blocking I/O that ignores cancellation, the task keeps running in the background after timeout. |
| **Leaking `CancellationTokenSource`** | Always wrap in `using`. Leaking it causes timer leaks. |
| **Swallowing `OperationCanceledException`** | Only catch at the orchestration boundary; use `when` clause to distinguish timeout from external cancellation. |
| **Uncooperative code** | If `work` cannot be modified to accept a token (e.g., third-party library), the work will continue running silently. For true hard-kill semantics, move work to a **child process** and call `Process.Kill()`. |

### Making Work Cooperate

For the timeout to actually stop work, pass the token through:

```csharp
// Before: uncooperative — runs to completion even after "timeout"
Func<int> work = () => { ExpensiveComputation(); return 42; };

// After: cooperative — checks for cancellation
Func<int> work = () =>
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 1_000_000; i++)
    {
        cts.Token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // checkpoint
        DoOneUnit(i);
    }
    return 42;
};
```

### Summary of Removed APIs

| .NET Framework | .NET 8 Replacement |
|---|---|
| `thread.Abort()` | `cts.Cancel()` / `cts.CancelAfter(timeout)` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Check `token.IsCancellationRequested`, decide to continue |
| `thread.Join(timeout)` + abort | `Task.WaitAsync(token)` |

> **Critical difference:** The old code *guaranteed* the thread died. The new model only *requests* cancellation — if `work` ignores the token, it keeps running. Design accordingly.
